flowchart TB
%% From a firing rule to a person, and then on to the agent. Two exits, deliberately different.

%% The paging exit wakes me. The agent exit only ever opens a pull request, so it arrives in the
%% same review queue with the same human at the end of it — just started without me being awake.

%% Everything between the two exits is a filter, and the filters are the design: an alert name has
%% to be on a short written list before it is worth an agent run at all.

  rules["A rule fires"]
  am["Alertmanager"]

  subgraph human["Reaches me"]
    ntfy["ntfy push<br/>critical only"]
    alerts["#alerts"]
    security["#security"]
  end

  subgraph second["Reaches the agent"]
    detector["alert-detector<br/>hourly, allow-listed names"]
    agent["Hermes<br/>opens a pull request"]
  end

  rules --> am

  am --> ntfy
  am --> alerts
  am --> security

  am --> detector
  detector -->|"one signed POST"| agent
  agent -->|"replies in channel"| alerts

  %% Amber is the only page: the push that means something is on fire right now. Blue is the filter,
  %% because the allow-list is the gate that decides whether the agent runs at all.
  classDef warn fill:#262013,stroke:#a98a3c,stroke-width:2px,color:#ebe1c9
  classDef control fill:#121820,stroke:#71b7ff,stroke-width:2px,color:#e7edf3

  class ntfy warn
  class detector control